before the break we heard from the  Chancellor Philip Hammond so let's get  some reaction to that interview we're  joined from some set by the conservative  honcho Jacob Riis mark thank you very  much for being with us  I know you've night to listen to a bit  of that interview one of the things that  really struck me from what the  Chancellor was saying was that if there  isn't a deal he said that he'd have to  set out a new budget next year and  actually strike out in a different  direction with the economy what do you  make of that it's not something you'd  welcome or is that something that  worries you well I would have thought it  would be better to take the  opportunities ahead of us now rather  than waiting this doesn't sound like  George Osborne's punishment budget do  you remember that we were going to get a  punishment budget  immediately after the referendum if we  had the audacity debate to leave there  sounds like a approach where policy  decisions would be made to embrace  brexit and I'd be very much in favor of  that being done on Monday rather than  waiting a few months and does it concern  you then because from what Philip  Hammond was saying there he was talking  about actually setting out some of the  more specific details in the spending  review next year you'd like to see more  happening right now and you worried that  the perhaps the budget might be a bit of  a non-event well with lack of a majority  I think the budget will not be enormous  ly radical I think that's pretty widely  accepted I thought a lot of what the  Chancellor was saying and your very  interesting interview was important and  would be beneficial for the economy but  I think we should be making  announcements now about how we would  deal with leaving on well trade  organization terms for instance why  don't we get rid of all tariffs on goods  that we don't produce in this country on  the 29th of March at 2019 to show that  we're open to the world that we're going  to be a global nation rather than a  peripheral European nation do you think  the Chancellor could be a bit of an ally  in this or do you think that he's  someone who perhaps is not seizing the  benefits of breakfast oh look the  Treasury has been the bastion of  remainer ism  since the referendum and indeed before  it came out with all these lunatic  forecasts before the referendum as to  what would happen  purely on a vote Tilly bear in mind the  Treasury said very 800,000 job losses  potentially just on voting to leave  before anything had happened so the  Treasury's rather embarrassed itself has  a lot of egg on its face from getting  its brakes it related forecasts so wrong  so far and I think there is an element  within the Treasury that is still grumpy  about breaks it and that's a pity at the  same time and from the Chancellor's  interview it may be that here's of  course I'm speculating here he may be  talking about actually seizing the  opportunities of brakes that if there is  a no deal as you would see seeds and  opportunities others would see and  perhaps trying to deal with some of the  economic issues of ranks it by perhaps  lowering taxes trying to attract  business trying to change regulations is  that something that you'd welcome or do  fear it may actually put off motors on  the center ground III think that  approach is something the government  should be doing anyway that we want to  be a competitive Latex economy open to  the world and it's really interesting  that the Treasury did a dynamic  assessment of the tax revenues that  would come from cutting corporation tax  and reduced corporation tax from 28% to  19% and has seen the revenue increase by  over 50% so lower tax rates can lead to  more tax revenue helping both the  economy and improving public services  now the Chancellor did talk about this  some time ago when he said one of the  options was for the UK to be more like  Singapore and although that's not a  perfect comparison because Singapore is  a small city-state there are lessons we  can learn from other countries about the  economic approach we could take look  what's happened in America with the  significant tax cuts a year ago and an  absolutely booming economy if you cut  taxation you can have more money  available for public services a lower  deficit and better economic growth so I  think that's an opportunity that should  be taken regardless of brexit at the  same time even if you accept your  assertion that cutting taxes may benefit  the economy in the long term which of  course lots of people would refute it  entirely in the short term that doesn't  work that means less money doesn't it  that means ending austerity may become  more difficult to do now and after 10  years of austerity many people are  absolutely desperate basically  for those you know belt belt-tightening  to be loosened no no dare I say there  are two things that mustn't be confused  here  one is the total tax taker and the other  is tax rates that you saw with  corporation tax which is a very close  example in time but you've seen it with  other rates in the past that a lower  rate can lead to a higher tax take  providing more money for public services  at the point at which you did if you  look at the reverse you will see that  the very high rates of stamp duty have  lowered the tax take so tax taken tax  rates do not go hand-in-hand and that is  not a long-term issue it's an immediate  issue and I would be advocating looking  at rates doing dynamic assessments of  the effects that changing rates will  have so that you get lower rates where  they work and will produce more revenue  which i think would happen incidentally  with stamp duty - and now of course not  long to wait until we know more about  what's in the budget we've had some  announcements around things like new  high streets around roads what would you  like to see in the budget tomorrow  well I'm speculating on what will be the  new budget 24 hours before it's out is  is a bit of a fool's errand but I would  like to see the transyl and make  announcements getting us absolutely  ready for leaving on the 29th of March  so as I said cutting customs duties on  all goods that we don't produce in this  country for example would be something  we could do  cutting the v80 rates on products where  we're forced to have v80 rates because  of EU regulations so that they would go  so what are we talking about domestic  fuel women sanitary products and solar  panels and things like that where  there's a clear advantage to not having  v80 on them now in last weekend's papers  we heard some pretty ugly language  directed towards the Prime Minister and  killing zones talk of the knife being  twisted I mean it was pretty lurid  violent imagery were you disturbed to  read the comments of some of your  colleagues and what would be your  message to them my message would be and  that politics can be discussed in a  robust but polite way that the Prime  Minister is very good at this she makes  her views very clear but is never  personally abuse  and I think that's how politics should  be carried out I'm afraid I think these  terms are rather juvenile and indicate a  lack of seriousness about politics that  politics is not war politics is a  democratic debate to try and carry the  country along with whatever policies are  being advocated and to talk of it in  terms of war zones and things like that  is frankly pretty silly and just before  you go and mr. Reese morgue and last  week I spoke to some young conservatives  in Cambridge and they were basically  talking about you and rather glowing  terms saying you should be the person  who should be the next leader of the  Conservative Party I mean would you rule  out running did you manage to find my  six children in Cambridge because I can  only think they'd be the young people  who would advocate such a view you're  very flattering but but but now my  ambition is purely for brexit to be  delivered it's not personal it's about  the policy being delivered correctly and  so I'm not a candidate there isn't a  vacancy and I wouldn't see myself as a  serious candidate way as a backbench MP

